{"jalapeño":{"id":"263ff5a8-3105-438e-a893-e0f7fb0824ab","word":"jalapeño","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"variety of hot chili pepper","tags":["feminine"],"word":"pimenta-jalapenho"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"variety of hot chili pepper","tags":["masculine"],"word":"jalapenho"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"For example, salsa and pico de gallo from Mexican cuisine sometimes include fruits like peach, mango or pineapple, balancing hot peppers like habanero or jalapeño with their sweetness.","ref":"2024 June 6, Brooke Younger, “What Is Swicy, and Why Do You Need It in Your Life?”, in Cozymeal, archived from the original on 15 Jan 2025:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[154,162]],"id":"1d33ef6c-30dd-49f1-b771-fd2f7869fe84"}],"links":[["chili","chili"],["Capsicum annuum","Capsicum annuum#Translingual"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"jalapeño pepper"},{"word":"jalap"}],"coordinate_terms":[{"word":"cayenne pepper"}],"categories":["English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English terms derived from toponyms","English terms spelled with Ñ","English terms spelled with ◌̃","English terms with quotations","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 6 entries","Pages with entries","Russian terms with non-redundant manual transliterations","Terms with Arabic translations","Terms with Classical Nahuatl translations","Terms with Danish translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Hindi translations","Terms with Japanese translations","Terms with Korean translations","Terms with Mandarin translations","Terms with Polish translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Swedish translations","Terms with Turkish translations","Terms with Yiddish translations","en:Peppers"],"glosses":["A cultivar of hot chili pepper, Capsicum annuum."],"tags":["error-lua-timeout"],"id":"296ed12d-8d4d-432c-a9b1-5d721c04b15c"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"jalopy":{"id":"d4011512-1776-4b47-98c8-3de0890fa5ad","word":"jalopy","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"old, dilapidated car","tags":["masculine"],"word":"calhambeque"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"old, dilapidated car","tags":["masculine"],"word":"chaço"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"old, dilapidated car","tags":["feminine"],"word":"lasanha"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Dean is the perfect guy for the road because he was actually born on the road, when his parents were passing through Salt Lake City in 1926, in a jalopy, on their way to Los Angeles.","ref":"1957, Jack Kerouac, chapter 1, in On the Road, Viking Press, →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[146,152]],"id":"e86f9613-04a0-4ccf-9b09-8c4df2ec17a6"},{"text":"\"We ran into some real excitement,\" Frank said, and told his father the whole story of Chet's missing jalopy, the wrecked car which they suspected had been a stolen one also, and the attempted holdup at the ferryboat office.","ref":"1959 [1927 June 1], Franklin W. Dixon, The Hardy Boys 01: The Tower Treasure, page 20:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[102,108]],"id":"dd8afca5-1761-4327-bdd5-e0c56edf005e"},{"text":"There's only a rough track and I doubt if that jalopy of yours will make it.","ref":"2003, Terrance Dicks, Barry Letts, chapter 22, in Deadly Reunion:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[47,53]],"id":"2c455213-1652-4858-9289-c484af74091e"},{"text":"Through most of those early years of restless travel, he dragged his two children and his long-suffering wife, Maryann[…] behind him like tin cans tied to the bumper of a jalopy that no car dealer in his right mind would take in trade.","ref":"2009 November 20, Stephen King, “Raymond Carver’s Life and Stories”, in The New York Times, →ISSN:",